1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,619
This is ECE 320, video 13. The topic is interrupts and exceptions in the five stage pipeline.

2
00:00:09,069 --> 00:00:24,215
After this video, you can explain precise exceptions and the commit point. You can show which instructions finish, which are flushed, and which P C is saved. Then we solve question 3 of the spring 2020 quiz.

3
00:00:24,665 --> 00:00:29,273
Start with a program. It runs i 1, then i 2, then i 3.

4
00:00:29,723 --> 00:00:42,502
An interrupt comes after i 1. Control goes to a handler, which is a routine outside the program. The hardware saves the P C of i 2, the next instruction to run.

5
00:00:42,952 --> 00:00:58,355
The handler runs i h 1, i h 2, and so on. At the end, it returns. The program restarts at i 2. This is the basic part: an unplanned function call, and a return to the point of interruption.

6
00:00:58,805 --> 00:01:13,034
Now the tricky part. The program did not call the handler, so it could not prepare. It saved no registers. So the hardware must save the P C. And the handler must save every register that it changes.

7
00:01:13,484 --> 00:01:17,985
The slides use interrupt as the general word. There are three kinds.

8
00:01:18,435 --> 00:01:31,705
A synchronous interrupt is also called an exception. It is tied to one instruction, for example an overflow. The faulting instruction cannot be finished, so the hardware must handle it at once.

9
00:01:32,155 --> 00:01:44,997
An asynchronous interrupt is also called an interrupt. It is an external event, not tied to any instruction. The hardware has some freedom when to handle it, but it cannot wait forever.

10
00:01:45,447 --> 00:01:58,525
A trap is a system call. The program runs an instruction that causes an exception on purpose. In RISC-V, this instruction is e call. The slide asks: why not use jal instead?

11
00:01:58,975 --> 00:02:13,887
A model answer. The jal target is P C plus an offset, so the program must know where the handler is. A trap uses the exception path. The hardware saves the P C and selects the handler address itself.

12
00:02:14,337 --> 00:02:22,827
Now put exceptions into the five stage pipeline. The slides call the execute stage E. In this course we write X.

13
00:02:23,277 --> 00:02:35,501
Each kind of exception is found in a different stage. A bad P C address in F. An illegal opcode in D. An overflow in X. A bad data address in M.

14
00:02:35,951 --> 00:02:40,367
Asynchronous interrupts are different. They can arrive in any cycle.

15
00:02:40,817 --> 00:02:59,441
This causes a problem. Load word number 1 has a bad data address, found in M in cycle 4. Instruction 2 has an illegal opcode, found in D in cycle 3. The younger instruction is found first. But the handler must see number 1 first.

16
00:02:59,891 --> 00:03:11,262
So the slide asks two questions. One: how to handle several exceptions in different stages at the same time. Two: how and where to handle external interrupts.

17
00:03:11,712 --> 00:03:23,403
The I S A defines a serial order. Instructions run one after another. So an interrupt falls between two instructions, here between i 1 and i 2.

18
00:03:23,853 --> 00:03:35,309
But the pipeline overlaps instructions. At the moment of the interrupt, i 1, i 2 and i 3 are all in progress. The interrupt line cuts through all three.

19
00:03:35,759 --> 00:03:49,327
The rule is: the interrupt must still seem to happen between two instructions. Older instructions finish completely. Younger instructions act as if they never happened. This is a precise interrupt.

20
00:03:49,777 --> 00:04:03,665
Here is a state that is not precise. i 3 already wrote x 5 plus 1. The handler returns to i 2, so i 3 runs again. Now x 5 has plus 2. The result is wrong.

21
00:04:04,115 --> 00:04:16,083
The answer to question 1 is a commit point. Choose it before the state elements change, so before the memory write and the write back. The slides use M as the commit point.

22
00:04:16,533 --> 00:04:32,895
Each stage can raise an exception flag. The flag does not act at once. It goes into the pipeline register, with the instruction. At each stage, a new flag joins the old one. So the flags reach the commit point in program order.

23
00:04:33,345 --> 00:04:42,199
Each instruction also carries its own P C down the pipeline. At the commit point, the hardware knows which instruction faulted.

24
00:04:42,649 --> 00:05:03,001
At the commit point, the hardware writes two registers. E P C, the exception P C, gets the P C of the interrupted instruction. The P C itself must change to the handler, so without E P C, the way back is lost. Cause gets the reason. Asynchronous interrupts are also checked here.

25
00:05:03,451 --> 00:05:13,883
Then the commit logic kills the instruction in M, and all younger ones in X, D and F. And it selects the handler P C for the next fetch.

26
00:05:14,333 --> 00:05:21,543
Now animate an exception. Five instructions run with no hazards. Cycles 1 to 3 are normal.

27
00:05:21,993 --> 00:05:31,316
In cycle 4, add number 2 is in X, and it overflows. The hardware only sets the exception flag. Nothing else happens yet.

28
00:05:31,766 --> 00:05:46,422
In cycle 5, number 2 reaches the commit point M. Number 1 is older. It is in W, and it finishes. Now the hardware acts. E P C gets the P C of number 2. Cause gets overflow.

29
00:05:46,872 --> 00:05:53,571
In the same cycle, the hardware kills number 2 in M, the load in X, sub in D, and or in F.

30
00:05:54,021 --> 00:06:02,618
From cycle 6, the killed instructions show dashes. Number 2 does not write back. The younger instructions change nothing.

31
00:06:03,068 --> 00:06:16,209
In cycle 6, F fetches the first handler instruction. So the older instruction completes, and the faulting and younger ones are flushed. After the handler, the program restarts at number 2.

32
00:06:16,659 --> 00:06:26,025
Now, priority. Case a: one instruction raises two flags. A bad P C address in F, and an illegal opcode in D.

33
00:06:26,475 --> 00:06:40,747
The fetch address was bad, so the bits that D decodes are garbage. The illegal opcode is only a result of the first fault. So Cause is P C address. Within one instruction, the earliest stage wins.

34
00:06:41,197 --> 00:06:52,183
Case b: two instructions fault in the same cycle. In cycle 4, load word is in M with a bad data address. Add is in X, and it overflows.

35
00:06:52,633 --> 00:07:05,668
Load word is older, and it is at the commit point now. So load word wins. Add is killed, and its flag never reaches M. If add runs again after the handler, it overflows again then.

36
00:07:06,118 --> 00:07:12,539
So, within one instruction, the earliest stage wins. Between instructions, the oldest one wins.

37
00:07:12,989 --> 00:07:23,379
Question 2: how and where to handle external interrupts. The slide answer: check them at the commit point. Here, a request arrives in cycle c.

38
00:07:23,829 --> 00:07:39,658
The hardware treats the instruction in M as interrupted. E P C gets the P C of i 2. i 2 and the younger instructions i 3, i 4 and i 5 are killed. The older i 1 in W finishes.

39
00:07:40,108 --> 00:07:50,689
In the next cycle, F fetches the first handler instruction. After the handler, the program restarts at i 2. This is the same path as an exception.

40
00:07:51,139 --> 00:08:09,699
A summary. In the cycle when the hardware acts, the faulting instruction is in M. It and the three younger instructions are killed. The older instruction in W finishes. E P C gets the P C of the instruction in M. In the next cycle, F fetches the handler.

41
00:08:10,149 --> 00:08:22,587
The hardware always acts at the commit point. So a fault found in F acts 3 cycles later. A fault in D acts 2 cycles later, in X 1 cycle, and in M at once.

42
00:08:23,037 --> 00:08:39,527
Compare a wrong branch guess from video 11. The branch resolves in X. Only F and D are flushed. The branch and the older instructions go on. No E P C is saved. F fetches the correct target in the next cycle.

43
00:08:39,977 --> 00:08:57,983
Now the spring 2020 quiz, question 3. Interrupts happen at a high frequency, so context saving must be fast. The context is the 31 registers x 1 to x 31. Normally, the handler saves them in main memory.

44
00:08:58,433 --> 00:09:09,163
This quiz resolves interrupts and exceptions at the W stage. The slides use M. The method is the same: one commit point, and act only there.

45
00:09:09,613 --> 00:09:18,659
There are five parts. Parts A and B have no cache. Part C adds a data cache, and uses the cache ideas from video 14.

46
00:09:19,109 --> 00:09:27,919
Part A. With no cache, describe one way to save and restore the P C when an interrupt happens. Pause the video and try it.

47
00:09:28,369 --> 00:09:39,932
Follow the slides. First, carry each instruction's P C down the pipeline, in every pipeline register, up to the commit point. Here, the commit point is W.

48
00:09:40,382 --> 00:09:54,291
At W, write E P C with the P C of the interrupted instruction, and write Cause. Kill the younger instructions in F, D, X and M. Then the P C select picks the handler address.

49
00:09:54,741 --> 00:10:04,939
To restore, add a return instruction at the end of the handler. It copies E P C into the P C. Fetch restarts at the interrupted instruction.

50
00:10:05,389 --> 00:10:22,199
Part B adds two instructions. Save context saves all 31 registers. Load context restores them. The register file gets two sets, set 0 and set 1. A bit s selects the current set. Now s is 0.

51
00:10:22,649 --> 00:10:32,825
The copy operation copies the current set s into the other set, s X O R 1. Now set 1 holds the same values as set 0.

52
00:10:33,275 --> 00:10:39,825
The swap operation changes s to s X O R 1. Now set 1 is the current set.

53
00:10:40,275 --> 00:10:49,235
If both come in the same cycle, the register file copies first, then swaps. This design does not support nested interrupts.

54
00:10:49,685 --> 00:10:57,514
Part B 1. How does an application benefit from save context and load context? Pause the video and try it.

55
00:10:57,964 --> 00:11:10,700
Without them, the handler saves the context with 31 store words. At the end, it restores it with 31 load words. That is 62 memory instructions for each interrupt.

56
00:11:11,150 --> 00:11:27,193
With them, the handler uses 2 instructions. The copy and the swap happen inside the register file, with no memory access. So each interrupt costs much less time. This matters because interrupts come at a high frequency.

57
00:11:27,643 --> 00:11:37,093
Part B 2. Which changes does the pipeline need, apart from the register file? Go stage by stage. Pause the video and try it.

58
00:11:37,543 --> 00:11:42,279
F needs no change. The new instructions are fetched like all others.

59
00:11:42,729 --> 00:11:56,767
D decodes the two new opcodes. It makes two new control bits: copy for save context, and swap for load context. The instructions write no register, so Reg W En is 0.

60
00:11:57,217 --> 00:12:04,939
X and M do no work. They only carry the two bits down the pipeline registers, like Reg W En.

61
00:12:05,389 --> 00:12:22,435
W is the commit point, so the copy or swap happens here. All older instructions have written their results, so the copy is complete. And if an interrupt kills save context, it has changed nothing. Younger instructions have not written yet.

62
00:12:22,885 --> 00:12:38,927
One hazard remains. After load context swaps the sets, the younger instructions in F, D, X and M have read the old set. Flush them, and fetch again after load context. This is like a wrong branch guess.

63
00:12:39,377 --> 00:12:48,572
Part B 3. The change breaks some existing interrupt handlers. Describe the problem, and how to fix it. Pause the video and try it.

64
00:12:49,022 --> 00:13:02,419
Trace the two sets. P is the program registers. Interrupt 1 runs save context: a copy, so set 1 also holds P. Then handler 1 changes set 0 to H 1.

65
00:13:02,869 --> 00:13:17,653
Now an existing handler enables interrupts again, and a nested interrupt comes. Handler 2 runs save context. The copy writes H 1 into set 1. The saved program registers P are lost.

66
00:13:18,103 --> 00:13:32,781
Handler 2 changes set 0, then swaps. Handler 1 gets H 1 back, which is correct. But handler 1 then swaps to set 0. The program resumes with the registers of handler 2. That is wrong.

67
00:13:33,231 --> 00:13:44,857
The fix: keep interrupts disabled from save context to load context. A handler that must allow nested interrupts uses store word and load word, as before.

68
00:13:45,307 --> 00:13:58,961
A second problem: load context restores all 31 registers. So a handler can no longer return a result in a register, for example after e call. It must return it through memory.

69
00:13:59,411 --> 00:14:16,627
Part C adds a data cache. It uses the cache ideas from video 14: blocks, sets, tags, and L R U. Acquire context reserves an address range for the context, and release context frees it. Pause the video and try it.

70
00:14:17,077 --> 00:14:30,154
First, the size. 31 registers times 4 bytes is 124 bytes. The quiz gives no block size. Assume 32 byte blocks, as in the lecture example.

71
00:14:30,604 --> 00:14:41,527
If r s 1 is a multiple of 32, the range fills 4 lines. Each line holds 8 registers. The last line has 4 unused bytes.

72
00:14:41,977 --> 00:14:54,094
If r s 1 starts more than 4 bytes into a line, the range touches 5 lines. So the hardware must handle every line from the first to the last address of the range.

73
00:14:54,544 --> 00:15:09,563
Now the pipeline, stage by stage. F needs no change. D decodes the two new opcodes and reads r s 1. It makes two control bits, acquire and release. The instructions write no register.

74
00:15:10,013 --> 00:15:17,522
X computes the start address. The A L U passes r s 1, as for a load word with offset 0.

75
00:15:17,972 --> 00:15:33,375
M is the main change. Here the instructions use the data cache. Acquire fills each line of the range and locks it. Release unlocks the lines. A fill is a cache miss, so the pipeline stalls, as for a load word miss.

76
00:15:33,825 --> 00:15:49,078
W does nothing. To save the context, the handler still uses 31 store words, and later 31 load words. They access the data cache in M and always hit, because locked lines never leave the cache.

77
00:15:49,528 --> 00:16:00,856
Now the cache. Take a 2 way set associative cache with 4 sets. Each frame has a valid bit, a tag and a 32 byte block, as in video 14.

78
00:16:01,306 --> 00:16:06,234
Add one new bit to every frame: a lock bit, L. It starts at 0.

79
00:16:06,684 --> 00:16:21,020
Acquire context goes through each line of the range. On a miss, it fills the line from memory. Then it sets L to 1. With 32 byte blocks, the 4 context lines fall into 4 different sets.

80
00:16:21,470 --> 00:16:31,795
The replacement policy, for example L R U, must never pick a frame with L equal to 1. So a locked line stays in the cache until release.

81
00:16:32,245 --> 00:16:43,360
Release context sets L back to 0. The line becomes a normal block again. In a write back cache, it is written to memory when it is replaced, if it is dirty.

82
00:16:43,810 --> 00:16:54,711
One more check. Each set needs at least one unlocked way. In a direct mapped cache, the other blocks of a locked set cannot be cached, so they must go to memory.

83
00:16:55,161 --> 00:16:57,913
Last, compare the cost for each interrupt.

84
00:16:58,363 --> 00:17:06,107
With no cache, the handler runs 31 store words and 31 load words. Each one waits for main memory.

85
00:17:06,557 --> 00:17:16,861
With part C, the same 62 instructions always hit in the data cache. If a hit takes 1 cycle in M, that is about 62 cycles.

86
00:17:17,311 --> 00:17:31,583
With part B, save context and load context are only 2 instructions, plus one flush. So part B is the fastest. Part C keeps the register file unchanged and has no swap hazard.

87
00:17:32,033 --> 00:17:40,289
Recap. One. An interrupt is an unplanned call to a handler. The hardware saves the restart P C in E P C.

88
00:17:40,739 --> 00:17:49,529
Two. An exception is tied to one instruction, which cannot finish. An interrupt is external. A trap is an exception on purpose.

89
00:17:49,979 --> 00:17:57,317
Three. The pipeline finds exceptions in F, D, X and M. So it finds them out of program order.

90
00:17:57,767 --> 00:18:04,359
Four. Precise means: older instructions finish, and the faulting and younger ones have no effect.

91
00:18:04,809 --> 00:18:16,308
Five. Carry the flags and the P Cs to the commit point M. Act only there. Write E P C and Cause, kill M, X, D and F, and fetch the handler.

92
00:18:16,758 --> 00:18:26,913
Six. Within one instruction, the earliest stage wins. Between instructions, the oldest wins. Check asynchronous interrupts at the commit point too.

93
00:18:27,363 --> 00:18:41,976
Seven. In the quiz, the commit point is W. Do the copy and swap there, flush after load context, and allow no nesting. In part C, lock the context lines in the cache. That is the end of video 13.
